// Max's base Passive - see docs/max-ascensions.md for the full design. Purely reactive: when Max
// takes Health damage (or, with the Unbroken Spirit upgrade tier of Blood Debt, Shield damage), the
// attacker becomes marked, granting the live +DamageBonus below. Killing a marked target heals Max
// for a fraction of whatever damage IT dealt him during the mark, floored at MinHealFraction of Max's
// own max Health. Vendetta Strike (the Dash Ascension) is the one deliberate way to mark an enemy
// proactively instead of waiting to get hit - a generic "any weapon hit also marks" hook was tried
// and reverted, since it made every enemy near Max get marked (and bonus-damaged) automatically the
// instant his auto-fire reached it, with zero player choice involved.

// Vendetta's own base tunables - seeded once by VendettaPassiveData.Apply, permanently bumped by at
// most one field each from future upgrades (Settled Score -> HealMultiplier, Blood Debt ->
// MarkDuration), same "upgrades compose onto one shared component" idiom Weapon.qtn's shared ramp
// pool already established. Cold/static - changes at most a few times ever, never per-tick - kept
// separate from RevengeTarget/StoredRevengeDamage below, which change every tick/every qualifying
// hit respectively.
component RevengeConfig
{
    FP HealMultiplier;
    FP MarkDuration;

    // Base Vendetta damage bonus (seeded to 0.15 by VendettaPassiveData.Apply) - bonus damage against
    // whichever enemy currently carries this holder's own RevengeMark, read live in
    // DamageUtility.ResolveOutgoingDamage rather than baked into CharacterStats.DamageMultiplier, same
    // "read an optional owner-side component, multiply damage conditionally" idiom Unstable
    // Targeting/Stun Damage Bonus already use. No DamageSource restriction, so it applies to weapon/
    // skill/dash/Burn-tick damage alike.
    FP DamageBonus;

    // Guaranteed minimum on-kill heal, as a fraction of Max's own MaxHealth - read in
    // MaxVendettaSystem.OnEntityKilled alongside StoredDamage * HealMultiplier and
    // EnemyMaxHealthFraction below (the highest of all three wins), so a kill on a marked target that
    // barely damaged Max before dying still heals something. Seeded to 0.01 (1%) by
    // VendettaPassiveData.Apply.
    FP MinHealFraction;

    // Second heal floor, as a fraction of the KILLED ENEMY's own MaxHealth rather than Max's -
    // read in MaxVendettaSystem.OnEntityKilled alongside the two fields above (the highest of all
    // three wins), so a kill on a genuinely tough enemy still heals meaningfully even if it barely
    // scratched Max or was only proactively marked (Vendetta Strike) with no banked StoredDamage at
    // all. Seeded to 0.05 (5%) by VendettaPassiveData.Apply - a decisive placeholder, not yet
    // balance-passed.
    FP EnemyMaxHealthFraction;
}

// Unbroken Spirit upgrade - tag only, no fields. Presence means Shield damage from an active
// RevengeMark also accumulates into RevengeMark.StoredDamage and can itself create/refresh a mark,
// not just Health damage (see MaxVendettaSystem.OnShieldDamageApplied). No PassiveUpgradeData grants
// this yet - added now purely so the base Vendetta system compiles against it; wiring the actual
// upgrade is separate, tracked scope (see docs/max-vendetta-fire-mastery.md's implementation order).
component ShieldDamageCountsForRevenge
{
}

// Lives on the ENEMY that damaged a Vendetta holder, not on the holder itself - this is what lets
// Max (or any future Vendetta holder) keep an arbitrary number of enemies marked simultaneously
// instead of a single "current target": ECS already gives every enemy its own component instance for
// free, so there's no fixed cap to author here (see RevengeMarkTimeoutSystem, which ticks every
// instance of this independently). Lazily added the first time this enemy lands a qualifying hit,
// removed the instant the mark is consumed (this enemy is killed by MarkedBy) or expires
// (RemainingDuration reaches 0). MarkedBy switching to a different Vendetta holder (this enemy hit a
// second holder) resets StoredDamage - a new holder's tally starts from zero, matching the old
// single-target replacement rule but scoped per enemy instead of per holder. Also doubles as the
// target-side display source for CharacterUiWidget's "marked" indicator, same pattern every other
// indicator uses (see CharacterUiWidget.UpdateExplodeOnDeath for the closest precedent).
component RevengeMark
{
    EntityRef MarkedBy;
    FP RemainingDuration;
    FP StoredDamage;
}
